Axial members, compatibility and load sharing (@mech-axial-members: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Torsion-of-shafts-and-the-limits-of-the-circular-model.lingua#r_21X60ESY01KMZF3R8J8HKM9194") { r_1VCFFVTFXWNAS0G6CJC2B8DVKN
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Trusses; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), chapter 4 and section 5.6. Reuse the earlier static free bodies and add deformation compatibility.

For an axial member with section force N(x), area A(x), modulus E(x) and imposed free strain ε₀(x), du/dx = N/(EA) + ε₀. Integrate along the member and enforce endpoint displacements. A sign convention must connect tension, positive displacement and the force on each cut.

Series segments without intermediate axial loads share the same section force; their compliances L/(EA) add. Parallel members constrained to the same extension share load according to axial stiffness, not simply according to area unless length and modulus also match. A thermal mismatch changes this sharing.

Two parallel bars of equal length and area with moduli 70 and 210 GPa carry a total 4 kN elastically as 1 and 3 kN under equal extension and no free-strain mismatch. Their stresses differ while the total strain agrees. If one yields, that proportional sharing changes.

**Work:** Derive a stepped bar with an intermediate force, an imposed support displacement and a heated segment. Check displacements, internal-force jumps and total reaction balance. Compare the exact piecewise account with the finite-element bar developed later in this section.

